\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Chordal Gaussian gluing along a junction tree}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A clique is a vertex subset whose distinct vertices are pairwise
adjacent; a maximal clique is inclusion-maximal. A finite simple graph
#{\Gamma} on #{V} is \newvocab{chordal} if it has no
induced cycle of length at least four. Suppose centered positive-definite
Gaussian laws #{\mu_K} on its maximal cliques agree on every overlap.
Then there is a unique centered positive-definite Gaussian law with
these clique marginals that is [Markov with respect to](fdip-0065)
#{\Gamma}. Uniqueness is asserted within that Markov class.}

\p{The maximal cliques admit a \newvocab{junction tree}: a tree
#{\mathcal T} whose nodes are the maximal cliques, such that the nodes
containing any given vertex form a connected subtree. This is the
running-intersection property. Write #{S_e=K\cap K'} for the
separator on an edge #{e=KK'} and #{p_{S_e}} for its common marginal
density. Empty separators have density one. The glued law and precision
are
##{p(\phi)=\frac{\prod_Kp_K(\phi_K)}
                       {\prod_{e\in E(\mathcal T)}p_{S_e}(\phi_{S_e})},}
##{Q=\sum_K\operatorname{pad}_K(\Lambda_K)
       -\sum_{e\in E(\mathcal T)}
                 \operatorname{pad}_{S_e}(\Lambda_{S_e}).}
Separator terms are counted once per tree edge, including repetitions
of the same vertex subset. For #{V=\varnothing} the law is the unit
mass and both products are empty.}

\proof{
\p{We first justify the finite junction-tree construction. A vertex is
simplicial when its neighbors form a clique. Every noncomplete chordal
graph has two nonadjacent simplicial vertices. Here is an induction
proof. In the connected case choose nonadjacent vertices #{a,b} and
an inclusion-minimal separator #{S}. Let #{C_a,C_b} be their components
in #{\Gamma\setminus S}. Minimality implies that each #{s\in S}
has a neighbor in both components: otherwise restoring that #{s}
would still leave #{a,b} separated. If nonadjacent #{x,y} belonged
to #{S}, choose shortest #{x}-to-#{y} paths with internal vertices
in #{C_a} and in #{C_b}. Each path has no chord, and there are no
edges between their interiors. Their union would be an induced
cycle of length at least four. Thus #{S} is a clique.}

\p{Each induced graph #{C_a\cup S} and #{C_b\cup S} is smaller and
chordal. If one is complete, any vertex in its component is simplicial.
Otherwise induction gives two nonadjacent simplicial vertices; at most
one lies in the clique #{S}, so choose one outside #{S}. Its full-graph
neighbors still lie in that same induced graph, so it is simplicial
in #{\Gamma}. The two choices are nonadjacent. In a disconnected
graph choose a simplicial vertex in each of two components by the
same induction; complete components pose no problem. Complete graphs
have every vertex simplicial. This proves the assertion and allows
repeated deletion of a simplicial vertex, a perfect elimination order.}

\p{Induct on that deletion to construct the clique tree. Remove a
simplicial vertex #{v}, and write #{N} for its neighbor clique. Unless
no vertices remain, #{N} lies in a maximal clique #{C} of the remaining
graph; when #{N=\varnothing}, choose any such #{C}. If #{N=C},
replace node #{C} in its clique tree by #{C\cup\{v\}}. If
#{N\subsetneq C}, attach #{N\cup\{v\}} as a new leaf at #{C}.
These are precisely the changes to the maximal cliques. In either
case the nodes containing each old vertex remain connected, while
#{v} occurs only in the new node. A one-vertex graph starts the
induction. Connecting different graph components in this way uses
empty separators. This proves the required running-intersection
property. The perfect-elimination characterization is also stated in
\citet{Theorem 6, p. 119}{grone1984positive}.}

\p{Now remove a leaf clique #{K} of #{\mathcal T} with neighbor #{C}
and separator #{S=K\cap C}. Running intersection says that
#{A=K\setminus S} occurs in no other clique, and that #{K}'s
intersection with the union of the remaining cliques is exactly
#{S}. By induction glue the remaining clique laws; a one-clique
tree starts with its prescribed law. The remaining law's marginal on
#{S} is the prescribed one, since it has the prescribed law on
#{C}. The [two-law theorem](fdip-0066) then glues in #{\mu_K} by
multiplication by #{p_K/p_S}. This proves normalization,
positive definiteness, all clique marginals and the density formula.
The precision formula follows at each step. Every nonzero summand
is supported on a clique, so the precision is zero on all nonedges:
the constructed law is Markov.}

\p{For uniqueness, #{S} separates #{A} from the remaining vertices
outside #{S}: an edge out of #{A} would belong to a maximal clique,
but no other clique contains its endpoint in #{A}. Any Markov extension
therefore factors across this separator by \ref{fdip-0065}. Its
marginal after removing #{A} is again Markov for the induced remaining
graph. Indeed the Schur correction from eliminating #{A} is supported
only on #{S\times S}, and #{S} is a clique, so it creates no entry
on a nonedge. Induction makes that remaining marginal unique; the
separator factorization then makes the full law unique.}
}

\p{The positive-definite completion theorem of
\citet{Theorem 7, p. 120, proof pp. 121–122}{grone1984positive}
characterizes chordal graphs by existence for every compatible
positive clique specification. The proof above additionally constructs
the Markov extension and proves uniqueness within that class. Bare
completion existence alone does not assert that all extensions are
identical. Even for two isolated vertices, \ref{fdip-006A} has many
positive completions and exactly one Markov completion.}
